--TEST--
A subscript whose base is `mixed` is answered from the base's runtime tag — the element, the absent key, the base that is no array, and all of it again under `??` — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/arrays`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/conversion` makes `mixed` the one unchecked position, so `$m[1]` defers
// not only *which* array is behind the handle but *whether there is one* —
// `rule:types/erased-member-access`'s deferral, one storage kind along from a member access. The
// four rows below are `nvs_runtime::helpers::value_index`'s, whose doc comment
// is their home; this pins what each of them prints.
mixed $list = [10, 20, 30];
mixed $map = ["a" => 1, "b" => 2];
mixed $text = "not an array";
mixed $number = 7;

// The row that answers: a present key, off a list and off a map alike.
echo $list[1], "\n";
echo $map["b"], "\n";

// A base whose *declared* type already answers agrees with the deferred read
// for the same value — the deferral changes when the question is asked, never
// what the answer is.
array<int> $declared = [10, 20, 30];
mixed $erased = $declared;
echo $declared[2], " ", $erased[2], "\n";

// The two rows that throw. A `catch` binding has no callable members yet, so
// each arm prints a marker rather than the message; that the throw is
// *catchable* is the claim — `rule:errors/propagation` propagates it by checked return like any
// other call, never through `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`'s fatal ladder.
try {
    echo $list[9], "\n";
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "absent key: threw\n";
}
try {
    echo $text[0], "\n";
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "base is no array: threw\n";
}
try {
    echo $number[0], "\n";
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "base is a number: threw\n";
}

// Under `??` both failures answer `null` instead, which is what PHP's own
// null-coalescing read does for any subject at all — so the same three
// spellings that threw above fall through to their default here, and the row
// that answers is unchanged by the `??`.
echo $list[1] ?? "default", "\n";
echo $list[9] ?? "default", "\n";
echo $text[0] ?? "default", "\n";
echo $number[0] ?? "default", "\n";
--EXPECT--
20
2
30 30
absent key: threw
base is no array: threw
base is a number: threw
20
default
default
default
